# std/process imports and runs on the interpreter and the native tier

Guard: `ProcessOptions`' default `Map.empty` crashed the whole module at
import ("No method 'empty' on NativeFnV(<native:Map>)") — std/process was
unusable from `.ssc` on the interpreter (found by busi's sites-as-files
git-backup slice).

Second guard (2026-07-30): the `v2` lane was added because the native tier had
no `exec` native at all. `std/process.ssc` declares the extern and int, js, jvm
and rust each implemented it, so a program that shells out passed everywhere and
died with `unbound global: exec` on the DEFAULT lane. Running the SAME case on
both lanes against one expected output is what makes that impossible to
reintroduce; the timeout / large-output / env-scrub behaviours a corpus case
cannot express live in `OsNativePluginTest`.

[exec, ProcessOptions, ProcessResult](std/process.ssc)

```scalascript
val r = exec("echo", List("process-ok"), ProcessOptions(None, Map(), None))
println(r.stdout.trim())
println(r.exitCode)
```
